    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65998.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "I've yet to hear of any business in Kentucky that's come forward and say, please have the tariffs. In fact, it's the opposite. So we have Toyota, we have General Motors and Ford, They do business between the United States, Mexico, and Canada to make these cars. Steel tariffs, aluminum tariffs affect the price of cars. Recently an article was published this week saying that some cars will go up on average nine to $10,000 per car. So none of the automobile manufacturers are for this. But above and beyond that, I hear the same from the bourbon folks. We have a lot of bourbon distilleries. We are the the state of bourbon, Kentucky's the place to go. We sell it internationally and many of these countries are now putting retaliatory tariffs on us that will hurt our bourbon sales as well. The farmers, we export 20, 25% of our farm goods. They're not for the tariffs as well. It's important to remember that when people say, well, we're going to protect the steel industry. So you can look at a steel plant and sell. They have 1,000 workers and tariffs could give them 1,200 workers. But there are 83 workers working in industry's buying steel for every worker that's making steel. So the math just doesn't add up.",
