    "turn_text": "no other than that twenty five percent on canada in mexico i predict will be a disaster not only for kentucky but for our country uh... we haven't tried this uh... you know the president loves to refer to mckinley i don't think even mckinley tried something this dramatic and so the president loves tariffs he is the greatest word ever invented But really we're in uncharted territory here. From a conservative, flash libertarian perspective, though, I'm not a fan of taxes. I want less taxes because I want government to have less of my income, to have less of my labor. I also want less spending because I want government just to involve less in the economy. But tariffs are simply a tax. When you tax something, you get less of it. So if you tax trade, you get less of it. But it's based on a fallacy. And the fallacy is that trade enriches some people and impoverishes others, that the equation is sort of unequal, that when we buy something from China, they get rich and we get poor. The truth of the matter is trades a win-win situation. And it always is because you wouldn't trade with someone if you didn't think you were winning. In fact, it's just one of these interesting things about capitalism is that all trades of their voluntary, both parties think it was unequal. Both parties think they won. If I buy a radio from China and I give somebody $20 at Walmart for a Chinese radio, I want that radio more than I want my 20 bucks and Walmart and China want my 20 bucks more than they want the radio. But we both come away. We both think we won when we trade. The president makes it one-sided and says, well, whenever you buy something from China, they win and we lose. Well, he's discounting the fact that the average shopper at Walmart says about $1,000 a year from international trade, maybe more. And by discounting that, he's looking only half of the equation, but it's a fallacy. And for decades, really right and left in most universities came to understand and in most economic departments, the trade was good. And the trade made us all richer. And now it's all being thrown out the windows. So I'm trying to be a loud voice for the prosperity that comes from trade, because to me, trade is just an extension of what Adam Smith talked about when he said that our prosperity was basically proportional to the extent of the division of labor. So as we spread the division of labor around the U.S., it's good. But as we spread it beyond our borders, we become richer.",
