  {
    "episode_id": 65998,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65998.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "You know, the thing is, is that they have outlined a certain amount of cuts, like, you know, one and a half trillion dollars of cuts. It sounds like a lot of cuts, but the proof is actually in the next bill. The next bill will be called a reconciliation package. It's different. It's confusing all the terms. Recision is a spending bill. The president sends back and we cut spending. Reconciliation is a budget bill. And it can cut mandatory spending or increased mandatory spending, which is also a simple majority. So there are two different legislative vehicles that only require a simple majority vote. But the proof is going to be in what they put forward. where I'm already disappointed and worried is that many of them said, well, we're not going to touch Medicare. We're not going to touch Social Security. And we're not going to touch Medicaid now. So if you take those three off the table, that's about 60% of all spending. And it's really probably 80% of entitlement spending and reconciliation is about entitlement. The battle's not over till it's over, and so I continue to fight. I voted against the Senate budget because it did look like it was not even false hope. It was no hope of spending cuts. Now we'll see what the reconciliation package is. I'm also not a fan of voting to raise the debt ceiling $4 trillion. I've never voted to raise the debt ceiling. I don't vote for the spending. If there was a plan in place where they said we're going to cut spending every year like my penny plan and balance the budget, I'd vote for that much debt. The debt it takes to balance the budget over a five-year period. But if they're not even pretending to balance the budget, they're going to borrow two trillion this year and then they want another two trillion for next year. I'm just not voting for that because that's not what I'm for.",
