    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65998.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I'm for making the tax cuts permanent. They've been in place five years. If you get rid of the tax cuts, it's a huge tax increase. So to vote against the tax cut is to vote to raise taxes $4 trillion. So I'm for the tax cuts, but if they marry them up with an increase in spending and an increase of the debt of $4 trillion, I'm not for that because I'm for the tax cuts and spending cuts and I'm not for adding $4 trillion. So as much as I'd hate to vote against it, I'm telling them they won't get my vote if they stick the $4 trillion increase in the debt ceiling on there. Historically, the debt ceiling has been increased. It's done every year, but it's done by the people who vote for the spending, the Democrats and about half of the Republicans, the ones I call the big government Republicans. To me, it's a day of shame. And they are rightly excoriated for being the ones who spend the money and being responsible for the debt. And that's why they get to vote to raise the debt ceiling. It always works. Now they want to spread around the blame to people like me who don't ever vote for the spending packages. And I just frankly am not going to give them cover by voting to raise the debt ceiling.",
